 ### Semantic execution assurance
 
-Strict MCP assurance never trusts `executionContext` supplied in tool arguments. A `TrustedExecutionContextProvider` must supply a fresh observation bound to the exact task, run, stage and revision; observation IDs are atomically claimed in the workflow SQLite database and remain one-use across server restarts and concurrent connections. Expired, mismatched or replayed observations fail closed. The packaged server does not fabricate host attestation: without an authoritative host adapter, orchestrated semantic planning returns `BINDING_REQUIRED`. Since v1.17.0 the Claude Code distribution ships such an adapter. A PreToolUse hook run by the Claude Code harness finds the assistant message that issued the exact `plan_workflow` or `record_stage_result` call in the session (or subagent) transcript, takes its model, takes the lower of the hook's reported effort and the effort recorded with that message, and injects an HMAC-signed `_hostAttestation` token bound to the tool, the canonical input digest, the task/run/stage/revision binding, the actor and a five-minute validity window (the server's maximum observation age). `HostAttestationProvider` verifies it and supplies the observation; the existing binding, freshness and one-use checks then apply unchanged. The provider is enabled only by `AGENT_GOVERNANCE_HOST_ATTESTATION=claude-code`, which only the Claude plugin manifest sets, so the Codex distribution keeps failing closed. The signing key is stored in the plugin's workflow SQLite metadata; the token proves harness observation, not isolation from a process running as the same OS user. Since v1.19.0 a stage result may carry its provider output by local file reference (`StageResult.outputFile` with an absolute path and SHA-256, `output.output` null); the server reads the file (16 MiB limit), checks the digest, validates the parsed JSON exactly like inline output, and stores only the reference. Claude Code also cannot resolve `$ref` in advertised tool schemas (referenced object fields arrive as strings), so with `AGENT_GOVERNANCE_TOOL_SCHEMA_PROFILE=anthropic` the server advertises fully inlined copies of every schema that uses `$ref`; validation keeps using the exact contracts and the default profile is unchanged. Legacy plans and receipts remain readable, but strict MCP claim, guarded start and semantic stage boundaries do not advance assurance-less legacy plans.
+Strict MCP assurance never trusts `executionContext` supplied in tool arguments. A `TrustedExecutionContextProvider` must supply a fresh observation bound to the exact task, run, stage and revision; observation IDs are atomically claimed in the workflow SQLite database and remain one-use across server restarts and concurrent connections. Expired, mismatched or replayed observations fail closed. The packaged server does not fabricate host attestation. `AGENT_GOVERNANCE_HOST_ATTESTATION` selects one supported host adapter, `claude-code` or `codex`; an unset or unknown value leaves the server without a trusted execution provider, so orchestrated semantic planning returns `BINDING_REQUIRED` with a `provider-not-configured` diagnostic. Each distribution sets its own value: the Codex `.mcp.json` sets `codex` and the Claude plugin manifest sets `claude-code`. Host attestation cannot share one server with the FlowMarshal A2 profile.
+
+The attestation contract is common to every host. The shared `issueHostAttestation` path signs one host observation for one exact `plan_workflow` or `record_stage_result` call as an HMAC `_hostAttestation` token bound to the host, hashed session, agent, turn and call identifiers, the tool, the canonical input digest, the task binding (bootstrap) or run/stage/revision binding (stage), the model, its model class, the reasoning effort, the actor and a five-minute validity window (the server's maximum observation age). The observation ID is derived from the hashed host/session/agent/turn/call scope, so signing the same host call again yields the same ID. `HostAttestationProvider` verifies the signature, that the token's host is the configured adapter and that the adapter maps the model to the signed class, then that the tool, input digest and phase match the call being handled; the existing binding, freshness, one-use and minimum-requirement checks in `WorkflowService` then apply unchanged. A call without a token returns `BINDING_REQUIRED` with the provider's connection diagnostic; a malformed, forged, altered, mismatched, expired or already consumed token, or an observation below the stage floor, returns `BINDING_INVALID`. Adapters own only the model-class policy and how the host observation is obtained; they hand that observation to `issueHostAttestation` and never implement signing, binding, freshness or replay checks themselves. The signing key is stored in the SQLite metadata of the workflow database the server opens; the token proves harness observation, not isolation from a process running as the same OS user. Hosts without hooks can supply the same observation through `HostAttestationProvider.runObserved` or the bundled `host-attestation-api.mjs`, as described in [host-execution-attestation.md](host-execution-attestation.md).
+
+The Claude Code adapter has shipped since v1.17.0. The Claude overlay runs `host-attestation-hook.mjs` on `PreToolUse` for the two tools and on `SessionStart` and `PostModelSwitch` to record the main thread's session model. The PreToolUse hook finds the assistant message that issued the exact call in the session (or subagent) transcript, takes its model, and takes the lower of the hook's reported effort and the effort recorded with that message. When the issuing message is not yet in the transcript, it falls back to the latest assistant message's model or, on the main thread only, a newer recorded session model, with effort taken only from the hook. Model classes follow the Haiku/Sonnet/Opus/Fable family.
+
+The Codex adapter runs the same hook bundle with `--host=codex` on `PreToolUse` for the two tools (`hooks/hooks.json`). It requires the hook's session, turn, tool-use ID, model and transcript path, reads `session_meta` from the first 256 KiB and the latest `turn_context` from the last 8 MiB of the transcript, and signs only when the session, turn and model match and that turn records a supported effort. Subagent calls, identified by `agent_id` or subagent metadata, are not attested, so a parent's settings are never borrowed. Only the exact model IDs in the Codex policy map to a model class. The hook returns `permissionDecision: "allow"` with `updatedInput`; when no token is issued, both adapters remove any caller-supplied token, and the Codex hook reports the reason in a `systemMessage`.
+
+Since v1.19.0 a stage result may carry its provider output by local file reference (`StageResult.outputFile` with an absolute path and SHA-256, `output.output` null); the server reads the file (16 MiB limit), checks the digest, validates the parsed JSON exactly like inline output, and stores only the reference. Claude Code also cannot resolve `$ref` in advertised tool schemas (referenced object fields arrive as strings), so with `AGENT_GOVERNANCE_TOOL_SCHEMA_PROFILE=anthropic` the server advertises fully inlined copies of every schema that uses `$ref`; validation keeps using the exact contracts and the default profile is unchanged. Legacy plans and receipts remain readable, but strict MCP claim, guarded start and semantic stage boundaries do not advance assurance-less legacy plans.
 
 
 MCP의 `plan_workflow` 경계는 `WorkflowService.planWorkflow(..., true)`로 execution assurance를 fail-closed로 강제합니다. 직접 programmatic 호출은 기존 내부 도구와 legacy receipt 호환을 위해 기본적으로 permissive이며, 실제 MCP handler가 strict mode를 선택합니다. ExecutionContext가 결속된 새 plan의 stage-level 검사는 그대로 유지됩니다.
